---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Why Cut Paths? — Cutline">
  <h1>Why Cut Paths?</h1>

  <section class="intro">
    <p class="lead">
      Many people believe Narge should not be easy to reach on the ordinary internet. 
      A cut path makes that belief actionable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Problem</h2>
    <p>
      The current internet makes Narge content trivially accessible. A single typo, search result, 
      or link can expose anyone to content from the <a href="/spec">Narge class</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not about prohibition. It's about routing. The internet can work differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is not a kids product.</strong> It is not family-device scare messaging. 
      It is a technical routing choice for anyone who prefers that Narge not be the default.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a Cut Path Does</h2>
    <p>
      A cut path is a network that does not resolve the Narge content class. Organizations load the 
      Narge list into their own DNS servers or firewalls, making Narge harder to reach—not impossible, 
      just not the default.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sites identified as Narge-purpose return no address (NXDOMAIN) or are blocked</li>
      <li>Everything else resolves normally</li>
      <li>No accounts, no web dashboards, no "family settings"</li>
      <li>This is a technical class, not a moral judgment system</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why This Approach</h2>
    
    <h3>Narrow and Technical</h3>
    <p>
      Narge is a defined content class. See the <a href="/spec">specification</a> for the full definition. 
      The definition is narrow by design.
    </p>

    <h3>Host-Level Intent</h3>
    <p>
      The classification looks at a site's purpose, not individual pictures. The <a href="/spec">spec</a> 
      defines what is and is not Narge based on purpose.
    </p>

    <h3>Open and Verifiable</h3>
    <p>
      The specification and list sources are all public. You can read exactly what is happening. 
      No company stamp, no secret rules.
    </p>

    <h3>Not Comprehensive</h3>
    <p>
      This is not a kids-safety product, an addiction treatment, or a house-wide solution. If you need those, 
      use a comprehensive tool. A cut path addresses one narrow class.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Not Government, Not a Brand</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a law. It's not a government program. It's not a certification or trust seal. 
      It's infrastructure anyone can run. The current goal is corporate IT adoption—companies loading 
      the Narge list into their own DNS or firewall.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Honesty About Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      The Narge list will not stop someone who wants to find these sites. It makes them harder to reach 
      by accident or habit. If you need a more comprehensive solution, use additional tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hostname list is an approximation maintained at 
      <code>dist/narge.txt</code> in the public repository. 
      Some sites will be misclassified. That is expected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <h2>Using Narge</h2>
    <p>
      The Narge hostname list is publicly available at <code>https://feeds.thecutline.org</code>. 
      See <a href="/for-it">For IT Teams</a> if you want to load the Narge list into your infrastructure.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  section {
    margin: var(--spacing-2xl) 0;
  }

  h3 {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: var(--spacing-lg);
    margin: var(--spacing-md) 0;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cta {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--spacing-xl);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-2xl);
  }

  .cta h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .cta p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
